Brief Overview

This work improves the OPA required AWS tagging policy by moving Project and Environment validation away from hardcoded values and making imac-config the primary source of truth.

The updated solution:

Dynamically loads Projects and Environments from imac-config stored in DynamoDB and passes them to OPA as data.json.
Uses the Jenkins instance role for DynamoDB access by clearing AWS_PROFILE only for the DynamoDB request, preventing pipelines such as SIE from looking for the configuration table in the wrong AWS account.
Validates Project and Environment dynamically so changes made in imac-config automatically flow into OPA without requiring policy updates.
Supports legacy Project names such as dwb → data_workbench and mle → ml-engineering, while still validating their environments against the dynamically loaded canonical project configuration.
Preserves the IMAC exception allowing a 12-digit AWS account ID as the Environment value.
Fails safely on bad configuration data by denying evaluation when data.tagging, projects, or environments is missing or malformed.
Adds unit-test coverage for dynamic configuration, legacy aliases, invalid environments, missing configuration data, IMAC account IDs, and environments such as dev-imac-lza.

Overall: the tagging policy is now more centralized and maintainable—imac-config controls the valid Projects and Environments, while OPA enforces those values and maintains only the necessary backward-compatibility exceptions.
